  "query_preview": "..An area of high pressure will continue to dominate\nthe Southwest, keeping mild temperatures and dry conditions in\nplace. This quieter pattern will stick around through Thursday,\nprior to unsettled weather returning by Friday through the\nweekend and into next week. \n\nThe previously mentioned area of high pressure will push eastward\nas a couple of disturbances move in from the Pacific. The influx\nof moisture we are expecting will come in waves, due to the nature\nof having two separate troughs impacting the Southwest. The first\nsystem is a shortwave moving in from the Southern California Coast\non Friday. This first pass will keep showers focused across the\nWhite Mountains and eastern Mogollon Rim due to the more southerly\ntrajectory of this system. Following right behind is a larger\ndisturbance moving in from the Pacific Northwest Coast. There are\nsome discrepancies between models on how far south this trough\nwill dig, so there is still some uncertainty with our coverage in\nprecipitation for Saturday and Sunday. Either way, snow levels\nlook to generally be above 8500-9000', so anticipate mostly rain\nover the weekend. Outside of the increased moisture, cooler\ntemperatures and breezy southwest winds are also expected. \n\nA brief ridge will build once again early next week, before more\nunsettled weather looks to revisit the forecast. Check back later\nfor more details on precipitation chances for next week.",
